Z||000000||Digisys||09-25-2001||07:36 AM||||I am having some valve troubles! (this is on a 1988 V6) I adjusted the valve lash by placing the No. 1 cylinder on TDC and then adjusting 1,5,6 intake and 1,2,3 exhaust (turn them 1/2 turn after feeling drag on the pushrod as I was turning them) I then rotated the crankshaft 360 degrees and adjusted 2,3,4 intake and 4,5,6, exhaust. I have just the bottom manifold on at this time. When I go to check compression I have none at the spark plug holes but I can feel air blowing out thru the top of the intake manifold. I know I am overlooking something here, I am beginning to wonder if I am identifing the valves correctly (exhaust and intake) when I am adjusting them. I have no doubts about the firing order, etc. Can anyone sketch me a diagram of the valve layout, I really doubt I have these confused (which is exhaust and which intake) but I can not find any other problems. PS. I have tried different adjustment for the lash from 1/2 turn to 1 1/2 turns. Any ideas ?||||reg||9
Z||000001||87GTZ34||09-25-2001||09:20 AM||||I replied to your 1st post but my ISP went down and it didn't make it. I will update that post again.  In the future, just reply to your own post with *bump* in the text area to move the original back to the top.  It is very annoying (to a lot of folks on the forum) to see the same topic posted multiple times especially by the same person.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||09-25-2001||10:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Digisys:<BR><B>I adjusted the valve lash by placing the No. 1 cylinder on TDC and then...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>...at the risk of telling you something you already know, to lash the valves, you want to start with the engine at the number 1 cylinder <B>firing position</B>. Just because number 1 is at TDC doesn't necessarily mean it's at its firing position.  <P>You have to turn the crank clockwise until you can feel compression at the no. 1 spark plug hole, and continue to turn until the timing mark on the crank lines up with the  0-degree mark.<P>If, like me, your arms aren't long enough to turn the crank with one hand, and feel for compression with the other, screw a compression tester into the no.1 spark plug hole.  As no.1 come up on its compression stroke, the needle will twitch to let you know.  Or, you can screw in a hose with a whistle on the end of it.       <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000003||Digisys||09-25-2001||12:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>I replied to your 1st post but my ISP went down and it didn't make it. I will update that post again.  In the future, just reply to your own post with *bump* in the text area to move the original back to the top.  It is very annoying (to a lot of folks on the forum) to see the same topic posted multiple times especially by the same person.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Sorry if it annoyed you , it wasn't meant to be. I am pretty new at this forum thing so please bear with me, thanks for the tip. I kinda thought people were ignoring me because I am fairly new on here.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Digisys||09-25-2001||12:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by Digisys:<BR>[b]I adjusted the valve lash by placing the No. 1 cylinder on TDC and then...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>...at the risk of telling you something you already know, to lash the valves, you want to start with the engine at the number 1 cylinder <B>firing position</B>. Just because number 1 is at TDC doesn't necessarily mean it's at its firing position.  <P>You have to turn the crank clockwise until you can feel compression at the no. 1 spark plug hole, and continue to turn until the timing mark on the crank lines up with the  0-degree mark.<P>If, like me, your arms aren't long enough to turn the crank with one hand, and feel for compression with the other, screw a compression tester into the no.1 spark plug hole.  As no.1 come up on its compression stroke, the needle will twitch to let you know.  Or, you can screw in a hose with a whistle on the end of it.       [/B][/QUOTE]<P> As I mentioned I have no compression to read, the way I am checking to see if No. 1 is in the correct position is to place a small screw driver in the plug hole so I can feel the piston as it is coming up, I place my hand on the rocker arms for No. 1 as I watch for the wide notch on the Harmonic Balancer as it comes around. If the piston is at the top, and both valves are not moving (closed) and the wide groove is lined up with 0 on the timing indicator then I believe we are in the correct position.<BR> I then turn it 360 deg. for No. 4 piston to be in the correct position.<P>||||reg||1
